the north las vegas police department is  under new leadership today we spoke  one-on-one with the appointed chief and  assistant chief to see exactly how they  plan to move the department forward the  duo also making headlines by becoming  the first black leaders  jacqueline gravatt  i am the chief of police for the north  las vegas police department feels nice  to say that  yes it's a new day for the north las  vegas police department jacqueline  gravatt started with the department in  2001 as a patrol officer and hasn't  stopped working to better her community  i believe that with  keeping the focus on what's important  you know quality of life for our  citizens being involved with the  community the mother of two has been  serving as acting chief since the  beginning of the year when former chief  pamela ojeda retired after more than 25  years although the crime rate is down 35  percent from a decade ago she says the  department will focus on recruiting and  community engagement we have a ground  breaking coming up for new area command  gravad is not facing this challenge  alone as captain michael harris moves up  as assistant chief and claims the work  to improving the department is already  underway we are  looking to  get to any and every neighborhood that  we can  to create not only the diverse police  department but a fully staffed police  department both seeking to diversify the  police department to reflect the  community they serve while also becoming  the first black duo in leadership we  want our kids to really see how involved  and passionate we are about  changing what the face of law  enforcement  is the police chief is also coaching one  of four city soccer leagues which her  two daughters are part of she says north  las vegas residents can expect more  police presence in the community to  strengthen the trust
 

